instruction_analysis:
The skill instructions total about 5,710 words: SKILL.md has 546 and the four references 5,164 (artifact-contracts.md alone 2,155). Their content is mostly record-keeping and disclaimers:
- About 88 lines contain prohibitions or negations.
- About 21 are explicit disclaimers of the 'cannot / does not establish / not certify' kind.
- Record and CLI terms (run, batch, patch, audit-seal, verify, ingest, read-many, report_anchor, context_read, support) appear on about 80 lines, 42 of them in artifact-contracts.md.

Research technique gets a single sentence (research-workflow.md line 43: search with variants and follow consequential citations). There is no guidance on:
- query formulation or iteration;
- how to split the budget between breadth and depth;
- which source types to target (primary, official, review);
- hunting for contradictions;
- parallel fan-out with subagents;
- time allocation or stopping rules beyond 'reserve time for synthesis'.

The SKILL body orders a fixed ceremony: derive questions with origin, seal candidates, keep four stages separate, use batch and patch, finalise before reviews, verify and finish. That loads roughly 7.5k tokens of compliance schemas before the first search. Fitting outcomes appear in the runs: VAFER's first web call came about 18 s later, it ran fewer queries and opens than its paired controls, support rationales were copied as boilerplate, and refs were planted only to pass verify. The instructions optimise for not overclaiming rather than for finding more, or more decisive, evidence.
